      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "General William Leslie Cazneau founded the Eagle Pass townsite in the 1840s, laying the groundwork for a future border hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Pass%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After a decade of lawlessness, the arrival of the railroad in the 1880s restored order and spurred economic growth in Eagle Pass.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eaglepasstx.gov/425/History-of-Eagle-Pass",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Eagle Pass sits directly across the Rio Grande from Piedras Negras, Coahuila, making it a key crossing point between the U.S. and Mexico.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Pass%2C_Texas",
